<h2>Updating Task Groups Over Time</h2>

<p>
    Your renewal process will evolve as you learn what works and what doesn't. When you want to improve your process:
</p>

<ol>
    <li>Go to the Task Group Administration page</li>
    <li>Select the task group you want to update</li>
    <li>Make your changes:
        <ul>
            <li>Add new tasks that you've discovered are important</li>
            <li>Remove tasks that are no longer needed</li>
            <li>Adjust day offsets to better match your timeline</li>
            <li>Update task descriptions to be clearer</li>
            <li>Reorder tasks if the sequence should change</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Save your changes</li>
</ol>

<p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> Changes to a task group only affect <em>new</em> renewals that use that group. Existing renewals keep their current tasks. This means you can safely refine your process without disrupting work in progress.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot showing the edit view of a task group with changes being made (adding a task, adjusting an offset). Include a note or callout explaining that changes only affect new renewals. -->

<h2>Master Tasks vs. Task Groups</h2>

<p>
    It's helpful to understand the relationship between master tasks and task groups:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Master Tasks</strong> – These are the individual task templates (like "Request Loss Runs" or "Create Proposal"). They define what the task is, but not when it should be done.</li>
    <li><strong>Task Groups</strong> – These are collections of master tasks arranged in a specific order with specific day offsets. They define a complete workflow.</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Think of master tasks as the building blocks, and task groups as the blueprints that arrange those blocks into a complete process.
</p>

